  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On June 8, IDF aircraft struck military sites in Iran — confirmed explosions in Tehran, Isfahan, and Tabriz (Reuters; NPR). Iran launched ballistic missiles at Israeli military airfields within the same 24-48 hour window, its first direct state-level response since the April ceasefire. US intervention produced a halt the following day. A further Israeli strike around June 13 led to the indefinite suspension of the sixth round of US-Iran talks, scheduled for June 15 in Oman. This 32-day catch-up run covers Day 103 (June 10) through Day 135 (July 12). → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>From June 17 to June 21, the diplomatic track reversed sharply. A 14-point MOU was signed on June 17 — initially via virtual format, then formalised in an 18-hour face-to-face session at Lake Lucerne on June 20-21, attended by VP Vance, Kushner, and Witkoff for the US and Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf and FM Araghchi for Iran (Axios; Al Jazeera). The document covered a 60-day ceasefire extension, Hormuz navigation rights, nuclear and missile programs, frozen assets, and a sanctions relief architecture. On June 16, US Central Command announced commencement of active mine clearance operations in the Strait (CENTCOM/DoD News). Oman hosted a first US-Iran-Oman trilateral on Hormuz management on June 29. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>Commercial transits through Hormuz climbed from near-zero in late May to 51-52 vessels per day on June 24-25 (IMF PortWatch), but never established a consecutive 7-day average above 50. Iranian maritime attacks on June 25 and June 27 reversed the trajectory. By July 5, daily counts stood at 34. On July 6-7, Iran attacked three commercial vessels transiting the Strait; the US launched retaliatory strikes against IRGC maritime assets. Transits fell to 17 on July 9 and to 11 on July 10 (Bloomberg; UANI). The UK and France announced a multinational mine-countermeasure mission — with Germany, Netherlands, Belgium, Norway, Italy, Japan, and Canada contributing — set to launch within days of July 6 (The National). As of July 12, that mission has not yet commenced. → supports <span class="cite">F10</span> without move</p>

      <p>In the Knesset, the dissolution bill advanced through first reading 106-0 in early June with all coalition and opposition parties voting in favour. Knesset legal adviser Sagit Afik formally recommended July 17 as the dissolution date and October 27 as the election date — the latest constitutionally permissible. Polls as of July 9 showed Eisenkot's Yashar at 23 seats, exceeding Likud at 20; the opposition bloc projects 69 seats against the current coalition bloc at 51 (Haaretz; Channel 12). Netanyahu began exploring adding Smotrich to the Likud reserved list amid reports that Religious Zionist Party may fall below the 3.25% electoral threshold. → moved <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:40%;">Escalation 40%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:38%;">Protracted 38%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:22%;">De-esc 22%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (40%):</strong> MOU breakdown probability 60% (1−F9) combined with active military cycle and 65% chance of continued Hormuz closure (1−F10) drives the escalation weight; a further IRGC attack or formal US termination statement would accelerate it.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (38%):</strong> Neither a comprehensive deal (F11=12%) nor formal war — Hormuz partially closed, periodic IRGC-US exchanges below the threshold of declared conflict, and Doha back-channel preserving just enough diplomatic cover.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (22%):</strong> Joint probability of MOU survival (F9=40%), Hormuz partial recovery to 40/day (F10=35%), and some deal progress (F11=12%), adjusted upward slightly for scenario-level co-occurrence among the three variables.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's factional landscape became the primary operational variable over the 32-day period. Mojtaba Khamenei, confirmed as Supreme Leader on March 9, presides over a state apparatus in which IRGC commanders operate with greater autonomy than in the final years of the previous leadership. Parliament Speaker Ghalibaf led the Iranian delegation at Lake Lucerne on June 20-21, establishing him as the pragmatist face of the new order. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The factional fault line became overt on June 30, when IRIB cut Ghalibaf's pre-recorded MOU-defence interview mid-sentence. The parliamentary media centre publicly condemned the cut as unauthorised censorship — a public inter-institutional confrontation documented by Iran International (July 7). Elements aligned with the Mojtaba Khamenei-IRGC hardline axis used state broadcast infrastructure to limit domestic distribution of Ghalibaf's defence of the diplomatic track. Iran International subsequently reported discussions of a potential IRIB leadership shakeup, which the Mojtaba circle is treating as a test of institutional control. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>The IRGC maritime attacks of July 6-7 — targeting three commercial vessels in Hormuz days after the UK/France MCM mission announcement — followed a pattern of hardline action against the Ghalibaf-Araghchi diplomatic track. FM Araghchi subsequently cited US retaliatory strikes as evidence that US threats impede a final agreement (Times of Israel, July 12). Whether the July 6-7 attacks were coordinated with the foreign ministry or constituted unauthorised IRGC action is not established in open-source reporting as of July 12. → supports <span class="cite">F9</span> without move</p>

      <p>NCRI's July 11 briefing documents mounting economic stress: oil export volumes remain below pre-war levels; sanctions remain in effect under the MOU framework; rial depreciation and food price inflation — 72% year-on-year in late May — continue. The Mojtaba-IRGC faction is oriented toward extracting security guarantees and international recognition from the US; Ghalibaf's team has negotiated primarily around economic relief and sanctions architecture. The divergence between these two tracks is the central uncertainty for the MOU's survival through August 12. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

